"""Literature evidence: Early Dynastic IIIa (Šuruppak/Fara, Abu Salabikh), Early Dynastic IIIb (Girsu, Umma, Adab), Ebla.
Format as in 81_lit_evidence_ur3.py. OHANE volume I has no printed page numbers in ShG's copy, so the citation gives the
section and the PDF page (printed pages to be added from the print edition before submission)."""
B='bartashEarlyDynasticNear2020'  # Bartash, V. 2020. The Early Dynastic Near East. OHANE I, ch. 8.
E=[
 # ---------------- ED IIIa
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='TERR',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.3 (PDF p. 487)',
  quote="the official archives of ED IIIa Šuruppag document the exchange of workers, messengers, and goods among six Sumerian city states: Uruk and its district/twin-city Kulaba, Adab, Nippur, Lagaš, Šuruppag, and Umma. These data allowed Thorkild Jacobsen to formulate the theory of the “Kengir league”"),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='TERR',score=1,stance='qualifies',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.4 (PDF p. 488)',
  quote="A cautious assessment of the data suggests that the transportational, diplomatic, cultic, and other relationships that existed between neighboring states connected by the same waterways may explain the nature of this “alliance.” ... In summary, political relationships in southern Mesopotamia during the ED I–IIIa periods were dynamic and unstable."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='HIER',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF pp. 498-499)',
  quote="Visicato reconstructed the household and power hierarchy at Šuruppag (ED IIIa), where the ensi wielded the principal authority. Three “houses” play first fiddle in the archives: the “Big House” (i.e., the palace), the “House of the Lass,” and the “City(-House).” ... The ensi managed foreign relations and trade from the “Big House,” while the “City” managed local affairs."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='FISC',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 499)',
  quote="this sign combination refers to kingal, “labor manager.” Together with heralds, he was responsible for levying corvée labor."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='COMM',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.3 (PDF p. 487)',
  quote="the official archives of ED IIIa Šuruppag document the exchange of workers, messengers, and goods among six Sumerian city states"),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='PAL',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6-8.7 (PDF pp. 499, 501)',
  quote="Many administrative offices center on the “Big House.” Most artisans appear to have belonged to the “City.” ... Among the approximately two thousand people mentioned in this archive, seventy-nine were “sons of the palace” and included officials, physicians, cupbearers, and cooks."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='TEMP',score=2,stance='qualifies',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 499)',
  quote="Considering the pattern in the naming of Sumerian temples such as “The House of the Woman” and their role as grain producers, the “House of the Lass” may have been the temple of the goddess Sud, the patron deity of Šuruppag. Viewed this way, the Šuruppag archives offer a picture reminiscent of the early Sumerian tripartite model elaborated by I. M. Diakonoff, with the temple and the urban community functioning as its main pillars."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='TCTL',score=1,stance='qualifies',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 499)',
  quote="The “House of the Lass” was a major grain production center with a close relationship to the ruler."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='PRIV',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, Table 8.1 and §8.1 (PDF pp. 475-476)',
  quote="Šuruppag (Fara): over 1,000 tablets: administrative, legal (sales of houses and fields), lexical, literary ... Legal documents record the sale and purchase of land, houses, and slaves by individuals, usually the highest-level dignitaries of the temple and palace households."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='BUR',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.1 (PDF p. 475)',
  quote="Administrative documents record the management of temple and palatial economies and make up about 90 percent of the extant written corpus."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='SCHOOL',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.1 (PDF p. 474)',
  quote="the discovery of literary and school texts in domestic quarters in ED IIIa Šuruppag (or Šuruppak; modern Fara) and Tell Abu Salabikh (probably ancient Ereš) demonstrates that scribes passed on their knowledge and know-how in their homes. This is in line with the evidence from later periods."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='SCHOOL',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.9 (PDF pp. 522-523)',
  quote="The southern tradition encompasses individual scribal traditions from local city states, most prominently Šuruppag, where the bulk of the evidence comes from. The northern tradition centered around the city state of Kiš."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='LAW',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 497)',
  quote="The ensi also functioned as supreme judge, especially in cases of capital punishment."),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='STD',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 499)',
  quote="Records from ED IIIa Šuruppag were dated not by a ruler’s regnal year as in the ED IIIb period but by the eponym of a prominent citizen. This “republican” trait in the political system of ED IIIa Šuruppag suggests that each ED Sumerian city state had its own political culture"),
 dict(period='ED IIIa',param='STD',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.7 (PDF p. 502)',
  quote="The use of standardized weight measures is documented in written records from 2800 BC onward ... There were several different weight norms, despite the existence of a single “correct” standard based on the practice of weighing metals."),
 # ---------------- ED IIIb
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='TERR',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.2 (PDF p. 478)',
  quote="Most, if not all, late ED “city states” were the result of mergers between originally independent, and often equally large, urban communities into a single political hub: a seat of a patrilineal dynasty of male rulers. The Lagaš state is the most well-researched example of this structure. It contained the cities of Girsu (at least 370 ha), Lagaš, and Nigin"),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='TERR',score=2,stance='qualifies',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.2 and §8.10 (PDF pp. 479, 524)',
  quote="the final decades of the ED period, called the “proto-imperial period” by Gianni Marchesi, exhibit an inevitable trajectory toward the unification of southern Mesopotamia under one dynasty ... confederations of city states gave way to proto-territorial polities by the end of the period.",
  note='Applies to the end of ED IIIb (Enšakušanna, Lugalzagesi); the Girsu archive that dominates the corpus belongs to the city-state of Lagaš.'),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='HIER',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.2 (PDF p. 478)',
  quote="It contained the cities of Girsu (at least 370 ha), Lagaš, and Nigin (Tell Zurghul, 67 ha); some smaller towns, such as Sirara; and the strategically placed harbor town of Gu’abba"),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='FISC',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.7 (PDF p. 502)',
  quote="Copper dominated the ED IIIa period; by the end of the ED IIIb, silver gradually became the leading form of “money” for sales and tax payments."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='PAL',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.7 (PDF p. 500)',
  quote="Schrakamp re-evaluated this evidence and concluded that temples in the late ED period were organizations responsible for managing the subsistence economy for the population. However, the palace embodied the “state.” It maintained the military, controlled long-distance trade, and interfered in temple affairs when necessary."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='TEMP',score=3,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.7 (PDF p. 500)',
  quote="The influential “temple state theory,” elaborated between the two world wars by Schneider and Deimel, saw ED Sumerian city states as theocracies. In their view, temples controlled all means of production ... Approximately eighteen hundred documents from the “House of the Woman,” the temple of the goddess Bawa in late ED IIIb Girsu, provided the data that underpinned this theory."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='TEMP',score=2,stance='contests',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 498)',
  quote="A re-evaluation of palace-temple relations in ED IIIb Girsu allowed Schrakamp to argue that temples maintained a strictly subordinate relationship to palatial centers. This conclusion challenges the traditionally accepted sociopolitical and economic dichotomy of palace and temple in the ED period."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='TCTL',score=3,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 498)',
  quote="Recent analysis illustrates that some rulers (ensi) at ED IIIb Umma and Lagaš had previously been administrators (sagga) of strategic temples ... one of the main temples in the city (Emunus, the “House of the Woman,” the temple of the goddess Bawa), was de facto the private domain of the queen."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='TCTL',score=2,stance='qualifies',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 498)',
  quote="It should be kept in mind, however, that the situation in ED IIIb Girsu under its last ruler Erekagina may have been anomalous ... But whether this model is also viable for earlier periods is a big question."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='PRIV',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.7 (PDF p. 508)',
  quote="A rich household in ED IIIb Adab consisted of a townhouse, 480 gur (115,200 liters) of barley, 300 sheep, two male and three female slaves ... These data allude to the virtually complete economic independence from the “great institutions” of temple and palace of these households, mirroring Schloen’s findings."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='PRIV',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.7 (PDF p. 502)',
  quote="Merchants were essentially private individuals and not “officials” as has often been assumed. Consequently, long-distance trade was not under any centralized control. On the contrary, palace and temple elites were clients of the merchants, not the other way around."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='BUR',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.7 (PDF p. 501)',
  quote="The archives of the ED IIIb temple household of the goddess Bawa (ca. 2350 BC) mention some 700 persons who belonged to different categories depending on the type of services they provided to the oikos."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='SCHOOL',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.9 (PDF pp. 522-523)',
  quote="Both regional traditions developed Practical Vocabularies, which were essentially scribal handbooks and included all words that were necessary to maintain the accounting of goods in the context of temple and palatial economies."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='LAW',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 and §8.8 (PDF pp. 497, 509)',
  quote="Severe indebtedness and deep social crises allowed some rulers to proclaim debt clearances (amargi) and “reforms,” such as those of Erekagina of Lagaš ... The inscriptions of the rulers Enmetena and Erekagina (especially the “Reforms of Erekagina”) portray the deep social inequality, injustice, and abuse of administrative power in the ED IIIb Lagaš state."),
 dict(period='ED IIIb',param='STD',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.6 (PDF p. 499)',
  quote="Records from ED IIIa Šuruppag were dated not by a ruler’s regnal year as in the ED IIIb period but by the eponym of a prominent citizen.",
  note='Regnal-year dating in ED IIIb is by the ruler of each city state, so it is a local, not a polity-wide, standard.'),
 # ---------------- Ebla
 dict(period='Ebla',param='TERR',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.5 and §8.8 (PDF pp. 494, 509)',
  quote="Eventually, Ebla consolidated more than twenty city states under its authority ... At the time of king Igrišhalab in the early twenty-fourth century BC, the Eblaite kingdom stretched from Carchemish to Hamadu (Hama)."),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='HIER',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.8 (PDF p. 510)',
  quote="Ebla’s kings delegated most of their administrative and military responsibilities to their viziers ... These dignitaries presided over “lords” ... of which there were about thirty in the kingdom of Ebla ... the kingdom of Ebla consisted of several hundred small settlements, administered by governors who appear in written evidence under the Sumerian logogram ugula, “overseer,” and collected grain and cattle for the palace household."),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='FISC',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.8 (PDF p. 510)',
  quote="administered by governors ... [who] collected grain and cattle for the palace household. This demonstrates the tributary nature of the economic relationship between the center and the periphery."),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='COMM',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.5 and §8.8 (PDF pp. 495, 510)',
  quote="the Ebla archives illustrate that classified intelligence delivered by messengers was as valuable as military power ... Male personnel consisted of about 40 physicians ... several dozen “commissioners,” more than 30 messengers"),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='PAL',score=3,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.8 (PDF p. 511)',
  quote="The presence of six hundred smiths at Ebla suggests that the palace was the hub of both production and redistribution ... In contrast to southern Mesopotamia, where textile mills were maintained by temples, the palace dominated the centralized production of textiles at Ebla."),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='TEMP',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.1 (PDF p. 474)',
  quote="Specialists usually classify them as either temple archives (typically in southern Mesopotamia) or palace archives (typically in northern Mesopotamia, i.e., Syria and northern Iraq)."),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='TCTL',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.8 (PDF p. 510)',
  quote="The viziers, the royal family, the superintendent of smiths, and the high priestess of the god Hadabal constituted the highest social class in the Eblaite kingdom."),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='PRIV',score=0,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.8 (PDF pp. 510, 512)',
  quote="The organization of labor attested in the Ebla archives provides an image of society as seen through the lens of the palace ... Trade by independent merchants was vibrant. The state was their biggest customer"),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='BUR',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.5 (PDF p. 492)',
  quote="In contrast to southern Mesopotamia, for which flamboyant royal inscriptions are the principal sources, the Ebla archives routinely recorded historical events in administrative documents."),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='SCHOOL',score=2,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.9 (PDF pp. 523, 525)',
  quote="The local traditions of the Syrian kingdoms compose the third group of ED lexical lists. Two earlier compilations of traditions acted as the source material, although they were adapted to the local sociopolitical and economic realities and, of course, the Semitic language ... School tablets from Šuruppag and Ebla provide exercises"),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='LAW',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.5 (PDF p. 493)',
  quote="The next king of Ebla, Irkabdamu, forced the unequal Treaty of Abarsal on the weakened polity. It provided Ebla with control over river trade on the Euphrates."),
 dict(period='Ebla',param='STD',score=1,stance='supports',key=B,where='OHANE I, ch. 8, §8.8-8.9 (PDF pp. 510, 523)',
  quote="Cuneiform documents are dated by their years in office [of the viziers] ... Scribal work included considerable calculation ... based on the sexagesimal ... system in southern Mesopotamia and on the local Semitic decimal system in Syria."),
]
